d to check that the letter sequence (P at the start, C at position 2, with A, O, P, V filling the rest) doesn't create any forbidden trigrams.\n\nWorking through it, P-A-C, P-O-C, and P-P-C are all disallowed, so I try P-V-C instead, which checks out since only P-V-R is forbidden. Then checking V-C-x options, V-C-O works since it's not on the forbidden list, followed by C-O-A (also fine), leaving O-A-P to close it out — and that combination passes too since only O-A-A, O-A-C, and O-A-Y are banned. So the full arrangement P-V-C-O-A-P satisfies every trigram constraint, and since the rack only needs one P (the other P being the one already on the board at the origin), this placement is valid.\n\nScoring it: P(1) + V(1) + C(3×4=12 with the bonus) + O(2) + A(1) + P(1) = 18. I consider whether a different anchor point could land multiple bonus cells for an even higher score — maybe reusing a high-value letter like Y(3) with its own x4 multiplier — but that gets complicated fast, and 18 is a solid result, so I verify the placement doesn't conflict with adjacent cells and confirm it holds up.\n\n",
